Overlay patch 0057: FOLD IN UPSTREAM LUS `G_SETTILESIZE_LERP` (the LUS half).
Upstream commit `c57da1b4` on Kenix3/libultraship ("Add G_SETTILESIZE_LERP for
constant-size texture scroll interpolation", #1141; cherry-picked to their main
as `eee464e0`). Taken verbatim onto our pin — this is upstream code, not an iOS
behaviour change, so there is no `__IOS__` gating and the macOS build takes it
too.

WHY WE WANT IT (same bug as 0056). The old scheme, `G_SETTILESIZE_INTERP`, bakes
ONE tile-size command per interpolated frame into the display list, each carrying
a pre-stepped fractional tile coordinate, and the interpreter picks the one whose
index matches `mInterpolationIndex`. Two consequences we care about:

  * Every one of those baked coordinates is a float that has already accumulated
    `xStep / interpFrames` additions, so the derived tile width lands on 31.9999
    instead of 32 and HD replacement textures rescale by 32/31 — the jitter 0056
    describes.
  * Display list memory scales with the interpolation frame count. A 120 Hz
    iPhone runs 6 interpolated frames per 20 Hz game tick, so every scrolling
    surface allocates 6x the Gfx words a 20 Hz run would.

`G_SETTILESIZE_LERP` replaces the whole scheme with ONE command (opcode 0x4a,
5 Gfx) carrying the two ENDPOINTS as floats — the tile rectangle at the previous
game tick and at the current one — and the interpreter lerps between them at DRAW
time against a new `Interpreter::mInterpolationT` (0 = previous tick, 1 = current
tick), which the port sets before each rendered frame. Display list size is now
constant, and the endpoints are exact integers-as-floats rather than an
accumulated running sum.

WHAT IS IN HERE (all five files upstream touches):

  * `include/fast/interpreter.h` — `float mInterpolationT = 1.0f;`. The default
    of 1.0 matters: any draw that happens before the port sets it (or in a
    non-interpolating path) uses the current tick's endpoint, i.e. exactly what
    a non-interpolated frame should show.
  * `include/fast/lus_gbi.h` — `RDP_G_SETTILESIZE_LERP = OPCODE(0x4a)`.
  * `include/libultraship/libultra/gbi.h` — `G_SETTILESIZE_LERP 0x4a` and the
    `__gDPSetTileSizeLerp` packing macro (8 floats across 4 following Gfx).
  * `src/fast/interpreter.cpp` — `gfx_set_tile_size_lerp_handler_rdp`, which
    reads the 8 floats, lerps each of uls/ult/lrs/lrt by `mInterpolationT`, and
    marks both texture slots changed; plus the handler-table row. (The bulk of
    that file's diff is upstream's clang-format realignment of the table's
    trailing comments, not logic.)
  * `src/libultraship/window/gui/GfxDebuggerWindow.cpp` — the disassembler now
    steps over both `G_SETTILESIZE_INTERP` (3 Gfx) and `G_SETTILESIZE_LERP`
    (5 Gfx) instead of walking into their float payloads as if they were
    commands.

`G_SETTILESIZE_INTERP` is NOT removed; it keeps working for any producer that
still emits it. Patch 0058 is the SoH half: it emits the new command and supplies
the interpolation factors.

THE ONE ADAPTATION ON TOP OF UPSTREAM (two extra hunks, marked `SOH_IOS`).

Upstream expects the port to set `mInterpolationT` immediately before each
`DrawAndRunGraphicsCommands` call. In this port that loop is `RunCommands` in
`OTRGlobals.cpp`, and overlay 0008's perf probe owns EVERY line of its body and
its signature as patch context — adding a line there makes 0008 undetectable on a
second `apply-overlay.sh` run and hard-fails the build. (Observed, not theorised.)
So the port hands over the whole tick's factors once, from
`Graph_ProcessGfxCommands` where they are computed anyway, and the interpreter
picks the right one:

  * `include/fast/interpreter.h` — `std::vector<float> mInterpolationTs;` beside
    upstream's `mInterpolationT`. (`<vector>` is already included.)
  * `src/fast/interpreter.cpp`, `gfx_set_tile_size_lerp_handler_rdp` — `t` comes
    from `mInterpolationTs[mInterpolationIndex]` when that index is in range,
    otherwise from `mInterpolationT` (default 1.0f). `mInterpolationIndex` is the
    counter `RunCommands` already advances once per RENDERED frame, so entry i
    belongs to frame i by construction.

The fallback is what makes it safe: any draw outside an interpolated tick — a port
that never fills the vector, a frame before the first tick, the gfx debugger —
behaves exactly as upstream's unmodified code, taking the current-tick endpoint.
Net effect is identical to upstream's one-liner; only the handover point differs.
These two hunks are NOT `__IOS__`-gated: the macOS build runs the same
`Graph_ProcessGfxCommands` and must take the same path, or it stops being a valid
comparison for scrolling textures.

Ordering note: this patch must come AFTER 0056, which edits the tile_w/tile_h
sites in the same file. 0056's hunks are far from these (line ~580 vs ~4790), but
the series order is what the context lines were generated against.

